=== task: mcq hard
title: Popped for good
prompt: In `window_max`, when `nums[i]` arrives, indexes at the back whose values are at most `nums[i]` are popped and never come back. Why is that safe?
* `nums[i]` is at least as big as them and came later, so it stays in every future window at least as long: they can never be a window's maximum again :: Right. Anything they could be the maximum of, `nums[i]` is too.
- Because they're smaller than every other number in the list :: They're only compared with `nums[i]`, not with the whole list.
- Because the deque can only hold k indexes :: The deque's size isn't fixed; it can hold fewer than k.
- Because popped indexes are added back later :: Popped indexes never return.
hint: Compare an old smaller value with the newer, bigger one: which leaves the window first?
hint: Can the old value ever beat the newer one?
right: An older, smaller value is "dominated": in every window that still holds it, the newer bigger value is there too. That's why the deque can throw it away, and why each index is popped at most once.
wrong: The newer value is at least as big and outlives the older one in every window, so the older one can never be the maximum again.

=== task: multi hard
title: The window deque
prompt: Choose **every** true sentence about the deque in `window_max`.
* Its values decrease from front to back :: Right. Smaller-or-equal values are popped from the back before a new index joins.
* The front holds the index of the current window's maximum :: Right. That's why the answer is `nums[d[0]]`.
* Each index is appended once and popped at most once :: Right. That's what makes it O(n).
* Indexes that have slid out of the window leave from the front :: Right. The oldest index is at the front.
- It always holds exactly k indexes :: It can hold fewer: everything dominated by a bigger, newer value is gone.
- Its indexes decrease from front to back :: Indexes are appended in order, so they increase from front to back.
hint: Values decrease from front to back; what about the indexes?
hint: Four of them are true.
right: Values decrease and indexes increase from front to back; the front is the maximum and the oldest; and each index is handled at most twice: O(n).
wrong: True: decreasing values, the front is the maximum, appended once and popped at most once, old indexes leave from the front. False: it doesn't always hold k, and its indexes increase.

